diff --git a/STAGE2-PROBE.md b/STAGE2-PROBE.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..4e9b990
--- /dev/null
+++ b/STAGE2-PROBE.md
@@ -0,0 +1,131 @@
+# T124 DRAM probe and DFU-loader route
+
+`DRAM_STATUS` is the first stage of a ShofEL-to-DFU route. It launches a
+small ARMv4T Thumb payload on the T124 boot CPU, reads the BootInfoTable,
+memory-controller, and EMC registers, and returns a fixed 68-byte report over
+BootROM EP1. It does not access eMMC. The payload waits for a host ACK after
+sending the report, then requests a reset back into RCM.
+
+If the report stalls, run the phase-by-phase diagnostic:
+
+```sh
+sudo ./shofel2_t124 --usb-port-path 1-1 DRAM_TRACE
+```
+
+`DRAM_TRACE` sends a marker immediately on payload entry, then reports each
+BootInfoTable, MC, and EMC register read separately. The host acknowledges
+every marker before the payload proceeds. Its last marker narrows a hang to
+the operation that follows; it also identifies a failure before the
+payload-entry marker. It performs the same gated 16-byte DRAM scratch test and
+resets only after the host acknowledges the final phase.
+
+If the BCT pointer or size is invalid, both probe paths stop before reading
+MC/EMC. On T124 the MC can be clock gated in forced RCM, and an APB read may
+stall the AVP instead of returning zero. `DRAM_TRACE` reports
+`not initialized` followed by `complete` in this case; `DRAM_STATUS` returns
+an incomplete report marked with `DRAM_PROBE_REGS_SKIPPED`.
+
+The probe only touches SDRAM when all of these checks pass:
+
+* The T124 BootInfoTable BCT pointer and size describe a range wholly inside
+  IRAM.
+* `MC_EMEM_CFG` and `MC_EMEM_ARB_CFG` are nonzero.
+* EMC refresh is marked valid, RESET is deasserted, and CKE is active.
+* EMC timing is not stalled and automatic calibration is idle.
+
+When that register gate passes, it saves four words at `0x80108000`, writes
+and reads four test patterns, restores the saved words, and confirms their
+restoration. This is the beginning of the future loader range. The test is
+skipped when the register gate fails. Register values and the test result are
+printed by the host command:
+
+```sh
+sudo ./shofel2_t124 --usb-port-path 1-1 DRAM_STATUS
+```
+
+Run from this repository after `make`, so `dram_probe.bin`, `dram_trace.bin`,
+and `intermezzo.bin` are beside `shofel2_t124`. Moth's phased trace reached
+the absent-BCT gate; the integrated initializer below subsequently passed its
+live RAM checks.
+
+## Same-session stage-2 path
+
+`DFU_STAGE loader.bin --confirm-meerkat-rev02` is the host command for the
+integrated path. Before entering RCM, the host requires the pinned loader to
+be exactly 415,088 bytes and to match SHA-256
+`8f46062f2d201824337093a1e4c154e3048c019b147930da35b9d62e00c5e689`.
+Inside one ShofEL EP1 session, the payload then:
+
+1. Reads and reports the raw PMC strap and decoded RAM_CODE. It stops unless
+   the code is 2 and the host sends the explicit profile token enabled by
+   `--confirm-meerkat-rev02`.
+2. Calls the vendored Coreboot T124 `sdram_init()` with the generated Jibo
+   Meerkat RAM_CODE 2 parameters. It deliberately avoids the broad
+   `clock_init()` path, which also changes USB PLL/UTMIP state. It requires
+   the expected 2048 MiB range and a passing, restored 16-byte DRAM scratch
+   check before returning to the receiver. It does not rely on the invalid
+   RCM BootInfoTable BCT pointer, which remains zero on Moth.
+3. Receives the loader in 4 KiB EP1 OUT chunks at `0x80108000`, requiring exact
+   size, address, ARM entry metadata, and digest. It verifies both the stream
+   hash and a second hash read back from DRAM, then checks the first ARM vector
+   word. Any failed gate stops before the image can be launched.
+
+The default build sets `DFU_STAGE2_ENABLE_LAUNCH=0`. It can stage and verify
+the image, then return the device to RCM, but rejects `--launch`. Moth passed
+the Meerkat Rev02 RAM initialization, scratch restore, complete loader
+transfer, and SHA-256 DRAM readback in a stage-only run. The separate launch
+path requires `make DFU_STAGE2_ENABLE_LAUNCH=1 all`, the matching host and
+payload, and the host's explicit `--launch`; it repeats reversible memory
+checks at the SPL stack and malloc ranges before accepting the final `JGO1`
+command. The launch and DFU re-enumeration have not yet been run on Moth.
+`shofel2_t124 --dfu-stage-capability` reports whether the host was built with
+the launch gate enabled without accessing USB.
+
+## Loader handoff findings
+
+NVIDIA's T124 boot-flow note says RCM often begins without a valid BCT, so
+SDRAM may be inaccessible until code configures it. It also says the Boot ROM
+executes the BCT-defined loader on the boot CPU; Tegra124 U-Boot SPL then
+starts the Cortex-A15 CCPLEX explicitly. Therefore the current RCM payload
+cannot treat `0x80108000` as writable merely because it is the loader address.
+The probe is intentionally fail-closed. A BCT pointer by itself is not enough:
+the EMC/MC state and an actual save/write/read/restore test must also pass.
+
+The ShofEL payload is built for ARMv4T Thumb and enters through the Boot ROM's
+Thumb EP1 helpers. The target loader is an ARM vector image. Its reset vector
+is the image base (`0x80108000`): the first word (`0xea000016`) branches to
+startup code at `0x80108060`; `0x80108040` is the default exception handler,
+not reset entry. SPL starts the Cortex-A15 through T124 `start_cpu()` and then
+halts the AVP. The optional launch thunk switches from Thumb to ARM, masks
+IRQ/FIQ in ARM state, and branches to `0x80108000`; it must not branch directly
+to main U-Boot at `0x80110000`.
+
+Static review of the exact local U-Boot source (`.build/uboot`)
+shows `spl_boot_device()` returns `BOOT_DEVICE_RAM`; the configured SPL loader
+dispatches that device to `spl_ram_load_image()` and does not select an MMC
+boot path. Its reset code skips low-level init, so it depends on DRAM already
+being configured. SPL uses stack top `0x800ffffc` and a 64 KiB malloc area at
+`0x80090000`; the launch gate checks these ranges in addition to the image
+window. The SPL does not need a valid BootInfoTable BCT pointer after the
+integrated `sdram_init()` succeeds.
+
+The custom `jibo_dfu_entry()` runs after normal U-Boot startup. In the
+recovery configuration (`include/configs/kein-baseboard.h`) it uses
+`CONFIG_ENV_IS_NOWHERE`, disables the normal bootcount hooks, initializes MMC,
+and reads the existing GPT to expose DFU entities; startup does not write the
+boot or environment areas. The DFU backend rejects writes through the
+whole-disk `emmc-*` and `jibo-dfu-v1` aliases. Explicit host DFU transfers can
+write the exposed partition entities, subject to the local backend's bounds.
+Those later user-triggered writes are distinct from stage-2, which only writes
+the verified loader into DRAM. The stage-only transfer has passed on Moth;
+the launch handoff awaits a hardware run.
+
+## Source references
+
+* NVIDIA, [Tegra Boot Flow](https://http.download.nvidia.com/tegra-public-appnotes/tegra-boot-flow.html): RCM/SDRAM behavior and T124 SPL-to-CCPLEX handoff.
+* ChromiumOS coreboot T124 [`emc.h`](https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/coreboot/+/firmware-nyan-5771.B/src/soc/nvidia/tegra124/emc.h) and [`sdram.c`](https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/coreboot/+/firmware-nyan-5771.B/src/soc/nvidia/tegra124/sdram.c): T124 EMC register offsets, refresh-valid bit, CKE/reset bits, and calibration sequence.
+* Android Tegra kernel [`tegra12_emc.c`](https://android.googlesource.com/kernel/tegra/+/android-tegra-3.10/arch/arm/mach-tegra/tegra12_emc.c): T124 MC/EMC register use and DRAM geometry reads.
+* NVIDIA U-Boot sources built with the local bundle: `arch/arm/mach-tegra/spl.c` and `arch/arm/mach-tegra/tegra124/cpu.c`, which show the SPL boot-device and Cortex-A15 handoff.
+* The exact generated RAM parameters and vendored Coreboot T124 code are under
+  `payloads/raminit/`; `raminit_run.c` documents the callable, request/ACKed
+  same-session init protocol.
diff --git a/common/sha256.c b/common/sha256.c
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..0c711ea
--- /dev/null
+++ b/common/sha256.c
@@ -0,0 +1,135 @@
+#include "sha256.h"
+
+static const uint32_t k[64] = {
+    0x428a2f98u, 0x71374491u, 0xb5c0fbcfu, 0xe9b5dba5u,
+    0x3956c25bu, 0x59f111f1u, 0x923f82a4u, 0xab1c5ed5u,
+    0xd807aa98u, 0x12835b01u, 0x243185beu, 0x550c7dc3u,
+    0x72be5d74u, 0x80deb1feu, 0x9bdc06a7u, 0xc19bf174u,
+    0xe49b69c1u, 0xefbe4786u, 0x0fc19dc6u, 0x240ca1ccu,
+    0x2de92c6fu, 0x4a7484aau, 0x5cb0a9dcu, 0x76f988dau,
+    0x983e5152u, 0xa831c66du, 0xb00327c8u, 0xbf597fc7u,
+    0xc6e00bf3u, 0xd5a79147u, 0x06ca6351u, 0x14292967u,
+    0x27b70a85u, 0x2e1b2138u, 0x4d2c6dfcu, 0x53380d13u,
+    0x650a7354u, 0x766a0abbu, 0x81c2c92eu, 0x92722c85u,
+    0xa2bfe8a1u, 0xa81a664bu, 0xc24b8b70u, 0xc76c51a3u,
+    0xd192e819u, 0xd6990624u, 0xf40e3585u, 0x106aa070u,
+    0x19a4c116u, 0x1e376c08u, 0x2748774cu, 0x34b0bcb5u,
+    0x391c0cb3u, 0x4ed8aa4au, 0x5b9cca4fu, 0x682e6ff3u,
+    0x748f82eeu, 0x78a5636fu, 0x84c87814u, 0x8cc70208u,
+    0x90befffau, 0xa4506cebu, 0xbef9a3f7u, 0xc67178f2u
+};
+
+static uint32_t rotr(uint32_t x, uint32_t n)
+{
+    return (x >> n) | (x << (32u - n));
+}
+
+static uint32_t load_be32(const uint8_t *p)
+{
+    return ((uint32_t)p[0] << 24) | ((uint32_t)p[1] << 16) |
+           ((uint32_t)p[2] << 8) | (uint32_t)p[3];
+}
+
+static void store_be32(uint8_t *p, uint32_t v)
+{
+    p[0] = (uint8_t)(v >> 24);
+    p[1] = (uint8_t)(v >> 16);
+    p[2] = (uint8_t)(v >> 8);
+    p[3] = (uint8_t)v;
+}
+
+static void transform(struct sha256_ctx *ctx, const uint8_t block[64])
+{
+    uint32_t w[64];
+    uint32_t a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h;
+    uint32_t i;
+
+    for (i = 0; i < 16; i++)
+        w[i] = load_be32(block + i * 4u);
+    for (; i < 64; i++) {
+        uint32_t s0 = rotr(w[i - 15], 7) ^ rotr(w[i - 15], 18) ^
+                      (w[i - 15] >> 3);
+        uint32_t s1 = rotr(w[i - 2], 17) ^ rotr(w[i - 2], 19) ^
+                      (w[i - 2] >> 10);
+        w[i] = w[i - 16] + s0 + w[i - 7] + s1;
+    }
+
+    a = ctx->state[0]; b = ctx->state[1];
+    c = ctx->state[2]; d = ctx->state[3];
+    e = ctx->state[4]; f = ctx->state[5];
+    g = ctx->state[6]; h = ctx->state[7];
+
+    for (i = 0; i < 64; i++) {
+        uint32_t s1 = rotr(e, 6) ^ rotr(e, 11) ^ rotr(e, 25);
+        uint32_t ch = (e & f) ^ (~e & g);
+        uint32_t t1 = h + s1 + ch + k[i] + w[i];
+        uint32_t s0 = rotr(a, 2) ^ rotr(a, 13) ^ rotr(a, 22);
+        uint32_t maj = (a & b) ^ (a & c) ^ (b & c);
+        uint32_t t2 = s0 + maj;
+        h = g; g = f; f = e; e = d + t1;
+        d = c; c = b; b = a; a = t1 + t2;
+    }
+
+    ctx->state[0] += a; ctx->state[1] += b;
+    ctx->state[2] += c; ctx->state[3] += d;
+    ctx->state[4] += e; ctx->state[5] += f;
+    ctx->state[6] += g; ctx->state[7] += h;
+}
+
+void sha256_init(struct sha256_ctx *ctx)
+{
+    static const uint32_t initial[8] = {
+        0x6a09e667u, 0xbb67ae85u, 0x3c6ef372u, 0xa54ff53au,
+        0x510e527fu, 0x9b05688cu, 0x1f83d9abu, 0x5be0cd19u
+    };
+    uint32_t i;
+
+    for (i = 0; i < 8; i++)
+        ctx->state[i] = initial[i];
+    ctx->bit_count = 0;
+    ctx->block_used = 0;
+}
+
+void sha256_update(struct sha256_ctx *ctx, const void *data, uint32_t len)
+{
+    const uint8_t *p = (const uint8_t *)data;
+
+    ctx->bit_count += (uint64_t)len * 8u;
+    while (len != 0) {
+        uint32_t take = 64u - ctx->block_used;
+        uint32_t i;
+
+        if (take > len)
+            take = len;
+        for (i = 0; i < take; i++)
+            ctx->block[ctx->block_used + i] = p[i];
+        ctx->block_used += take;
+        p += take;
+        len -= take;
+        if (ctx->block_used == 64u) {
+            transform(ctx, ctx->block);
+            ctx->block_used = 0;
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+void sha256_final(struct sha256_ctx *ctx, uint8_t digest[32])
+{
+    uint32_t i;
+    uint64_t bits = ctx->bit_count;
+
+    ctx->block[ctx->block_used++] = 0x80;
+    if (ctx->block_used > 56u) {
+        while (ctx->block_used < 64u)
+            ctx->block[ctx->block_used++] = 0;
+        transform(ctx, ctx->block);
+        ctx->block_used = 0;
+    }
+    while (ctx->block_used < 56u)
+        ctx->block[ctx->block_used++] = 0;
+    for (i = 0; i < 8; i++)
+        ctx->block[63u - i] = (uint8_t)(bits >> (i * 8u));
+    transform(ctx, ctx->block);
+    for (i = 0; i < 8; i++)
+        store_be32(digest + i * 4u, ctx->state[i]);
+}
